   "start_page": 243,
   "summary": "This concluding portion of the Embassy's July 1938 report details American diplomatic representations regarding attacks on U.S. nationals, including the slapping of Dr. J. C. Thomson at Nanking and bombings of American mission properties, as well as the private visit of Philippine President Manuel Quezon. It covers the Japanese military offensive toward Hankow—notably the capture of Kiukiang—and severe border fighting with Soviet forces at Changkufeng, alongside diplomatic developments concerning Great Britain, France's occupation of the Paracel Islands, and trade pacts with Australia and Italy. Domestic developments reviewed include cabinet reorganization discussions, emergency economic controls including the import-export link system, severe flooding in Kobe, and the decision to cancel the 1940 Tokyo Olympic Games. The document ends with comprehensive lists of despatches (Nos. 3060–3162) and telegrams (Nos. 432–506) sent to Washington during July 1938.",
