   "start_page": 276,
   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits Report No. 143 surveying developments in Japan and the Sino-Japanese conflict during August 1938. The report details vigorous American protests over the Japanese naval air attack on a commercial CNAC passenger plane, Japanese refusal to permit the U.S.S. Monocacy to sail down the Yangtze River, mission bombings at Wuchang, and trade restrictions in North China. It reviews the Japanese multi-pronged advance on Hankow, persistent Chinese guerrilla resistance, the conclusion of a border truce with the Soviet Union at Changkufeng, and British representations regarding Kiukiang and the Chinese Maritime Customs. Internal political developments analyzed include movements toward a single national party, inter-ministerial debates over a Central Organ for China Affairs, and expanding national mobilization measures including the compulsory registration of medical professionals.",
